\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Positivity and trace class of the gauge transfer operator}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [spatial transfer data](fdip-007Z), #{C_k} and
#{T=M_bC_kM_b} are bounded, self-adjoint, positive, injective
and trace class. Both commute with every gauge operator #{R_h}.
If
##{\kappa=\sum_\rho d_\rho^2a_\rho=k(1),}
then
##{\operatorname{Tr}C_k=\kappa^{|E_s|},\qquad
 \operatorname{Tr}T\le\|b\|_\infty^2\kappa^{|E_s|}.}
Positive means #{\langle f,Tf\rangle\ge0}; in fact this pairing
is strictly positive for every nonzero #{f}. It does not assert a
uniform positive lower bound on the spectrum.}

\proof{
\p{For one edge, use the normalized matrix-entry basis
#{\phi_{\rho ij}(U)=\sqrt{d_\rho}\rho(U)_{ij}} of the
[Peter–Weyl theorem](fdip-007C). Unitarity gives
##{k(UU'^{-1})=\sum_{\rho,i,j}
 a_\rho\phi_{\rho ij}(U)\overline{\phi_{\rho ij}(U')}.}
Indeed, the matrix-entry sum is
#{d_\rho\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(U)\rho(U')^*)}.
Schur orthogonality therefore makes convolution act by
#{a_\rho} on this block. The tensor products of these bases over
the finitely many edges give an orthonormal basis #{(\phi_I)}
of #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}}, with eigenvalues
##{\alpha_I=\prod_{e\in E_s}a_{\rho_e}>0,\qquad
 \sum_I\alpha_I=\kappa^{|E_s|}<\infty.}
The operator assertions about #{C_k}, including injectivity and
its trace, follow directly from this complete diagonalization.
Here trace class for a positive compact operator has the meaning
used in the [Gaussian spectrum](fdip-007U): its eigenvalues are
summable.}

\p{For clarity, write the rank-one operator
#{|v\rangle\langle v|} as #{f\mapsto v\langle v,f\rangle},
with the inner product linear in its second variable. Then
##{T=\sum_I\alpha_I
 |b\phi_I\rangle\langle b\phi_I|.}
Each summand is positive and its trace norm is
#{\alpha_I\|b\phi_I\|_2^2}. Their sum is at most
#{\|b\|_\infty^2\sum_I\alpha_I}, so the series converges in
trace norm and proves positivity, self-adjointness, compactness
and the trace bound. For #{f\ne0}, bounded invertibility of
#{M_b} gives #{bf\ne0}; hence
##{\langle f,Tf\rangle=\langle bf,C_kbf\rangle>0.}
This proves injectivity as well.}

\p{Under a simultaneous gauge change of #{U,U'},
##{(h\cdot U)_e(h\cdot U')_e^{-1}
 =h_{t(e)}(U_eU_e'^{-1})h_{t(e)}^{-1}.}
Centrality of #{k} and invariance of product Haar measure show
#{C_kR_h=R_hC_k}. Gauge invariance of #{b} gives the same
commutation for #{M_b} and #{T}.}
}

\p{The coefficient calculation is a direct application of
Peter–Weyl orthogonality. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.peterWeylBlockAveraging_eq_starProjection}
identifies character averaging with the corresponding orthogonal
block projection. Its kernel is #{d_\rho\overline{\chi_\rho}}
and its matrix-coefficient convention is the conjugated one
explained in \ref{fdip-007C}. Reindexing by dual representations
aligns the conventions; reality and inversion symmetry give
#{a_{\overline\rho}=a_\rho}. This correspondence concerns the
block projection, while the positive weighted sum and spatial
multipliers are proved above.}

\p{The factorization proof follows the idea of
\citet{Proposition 1, p. 288}{luscher1977transfer}, under the
explicit pure-gauge hypotheses of \ref{fdip-007Z}.}
